//! THE ROUTER — a route is a GRAMMAR over the request target, and a
//! handler keeps its own typed record. The framer's boundary is the
//! contract: the target arrives already framed, its CTL and SP
//! refused, and the router never re-reads the head — nor converts it,
//! since a grammar reads octets and the span is what the framer left.
//!
//! THE TABLE IS COMPILED ONCE, INTO AN INDEX AND NEVER A POLICY. A
//! trie keyed on the hash of each literal segment narrows what a
//! target could match; the grammar reader still decides, and
//! SPECIFICITY decides which of the routes that answer does. So the
//! table answers exactly what a scan down the same list answers —
//! route for route, on every target — while costing the same whether
//! the list holds three routes or three hundred.
//!
//! A ROUTE'S SPECIFICITY IS ITS PATTERN'S, SEGMENT BY SEGMENT from the
//! first: a literal above a hole always, and a hole's rank is its
//! type's — `{id: int}` above `{slug}` above `{path...}`. Written order
//! breaks only a true tie, so embedding a list of routes never changes
//! which one answers.
//!
//! A HOLE IS ONE SEGMENT, and the table holds it structurally: a
//! route is reached only at the depth its own pattern has, so
//! `/ideas/{id}` is never offered `/ideas/7/extra` and cannot bind a
//! capture that invented a segment.
//!
//! UNLESS THE HOLE SAYS OTHERWISE, and it says so in the format's own
//! words. `{name...}` is the GREEDY mark — the hole takes its literal
//! at the LAST occurrence rather than the first — and on a final hole
//! the two are the same position, because a suffix anchor already
//! fixes that hole's end. So the mark STATES what the hole does
//! rather than changing it, `tailed` declares the route that carries
//! it, and the pattern reads exactly as its grammar does. `faults`
//! names a door and a pattern that disagree.
//!
//! A ROUTE WRITTEN AS A DECLARATION — `get "/static/{path...}" { … }`
//! — has no door but its pattern, so there the mark DECLARES: the
//! components come through `declared`, which reads the reach off it.
//!
//! A CAPTURE HOLDS RAW OCTETS. Nothing is percent-decoded, so `%2F`
//! is not a separator and `/ideas/{id}` binds `a%2Fb` whole. A
//! handler that means to use a capture as a path decodes it itself,
//! through `@std/url`'s `decoded`, and judges what comes out — the
//! router hands over what was sent.
//!
//! THE QUERY IS THE REQUEST'S, NOT THE ROUTE'S. A grammar is
//! POSITIONAL and ORDERED; a query is KEYED and order-independent, so
//! `"/search?q={q}"` would swallow `&page=2` into its capture and
//! refuse `?page=2&q=cats`, the same request written the other way
//! round. A ROUTE MATCHES THE PATH — `Request.path()`, the PATH
//! COMPONENT of the target in whichever form it arrived — and the
//! handler reads `Request.query()` beside it. A router that matched
//! the whole target would let a `/` inside `?redirect=/home` add a
//! segment and make its own route vanish, and would answer `/p` and
//! `http://h/p` from two different routes for one resource.
//!
//! A TARGET THAT NAMES NO RESOURCE IS NOT ROUTED. `OPTIONS *` asks
//! about the server and CONNECT's target is a tunnel's destination, so
//! `path()` answers absent for both and the table is never walked —
//! a 404, which is the same value a target nothing answers earns.
//!
//! ONE RECORD TYPE PER ROUTE, AND ONE PLACE IT IS ERASED. `routed` is
//! generic over the record; the table holds the erased handler, so
//! routes whose records differ live in one list while every handler
//! takes a `Request<R>` — the request, its route's record at `params`.
use @std.http_message.message.{Method}
use @std.meta.{Code, Format}
use client.{method_word}
use http.{Request, Unrouted, Response, Respond, status, separates, falls_under}

/// HOW FAR A ROUTE REACHES, and what its pattern's braces mean. A
/// DECLARED thing in every case: the door a route came through says
/// which of these it is, and nothing is inferred from the text.
export enum Reach {
    /// A static path — the pattern IS the target, brace for brace. A
    /// path may legally contain braces, and reading one as a hole
    /// would have the table key on nothing and the law cry wolf.
    Literal
    /// A grammar, matching targets of its own width exactly. A hole
    /// is one segment.
    Width
    /// A grammar whose last hole takes the REST: it matches targets
    /// of its own width and every width beyond.
    Rest
}

/// One route: the method it answers, the pattern it was declared
/// with — kept so a route an earlier one already answers can be
/// named — how far it reaches, and its handler, with the record type
/// already erased.
export type Route = { method: Method, pattern: string, reach: Reach, serve: fn(Request<Unrouted>, Bytes) -> Response? }

/// Whether a `{` in this pattern opens a hole or is an octet.
fn holes_bind(reach: Reach) -> bool {
    match reach { .Literal -> false, .Width or .Rest -> true }
}

/// Whether this route's last hole takes the remaining segments.
fn takes_rest(reach: Reach) -> bool {
    match reach { .Literal or .Width -> false, .Rest -> true }
}

/// A route from a grammar's reader and a TYPED handler. ABSENCE from
/// `serve` means THIS ROUTE DOES NOT ANSWER THAT TARGET; a 404 the
/// handler chose is a Response like any other, and the two must not
/// be the same value.
export fn routed<R>(
    method: Method,
    pattern: string,
    read: fn(Bytes) -> R?,
    serve: fn(Request<R>) -> Response,
) -> Route {
    Route {
        method: method,
        pattern: pattern,
        reach: .Width,
        serve: (q: Request<Unrouted>, t: Bytes) -> responded(read(t), q, serve),
    }
}

/// A route with NO HOLES — `/health`, `/metrics`, `/`. A grammar
/// names the fields its holes bind and refuses to bind none, so a
/// static path has no grammar and needs none: its reader is an octet
/// compare against the path it was declared with, and its handler
/// takes no record because there is nothing to read.
export fn fixed(method: Method, path: string, serve: Handler) -> Route {
    let want = path.bytes()
    Route {
        method: method,
        pattern: path,
        reach: .Literal,
        serve: (q: Request<Unrouted>, t: Bytes) -> if t == want { serve(q) } else { null },
    }
}

/// A ROUTE WHOSE LAST HOLE TAKES THE REST — `/static/{path...}`
/// given `/static/css/app.css` binds `css/app.css`. It matches
/// targets of its own width and every width beyond.
///
/// THE DOOR DECLARES AND THE PATTERN STATES, and `faults` names a
/// disagreement. `...` is the format's own GREEDY mark, meaning the
/// hole takes its literal at the LAST occurrence rather than the
/// first; on a final hole the two are the same position, because a
/// suffix anchor already fixes that hole's end. So the mark states
/// what the hole does instead of changing it, the pattern reads
/// EXACTLY as its grammar does, and the router is not reading a
/// marker only it understands.
export fn tailed<R>(
    method: Method,
    pattern: string,
    read: fn(Bytes) -> R?,
    serve: fn(Request<R>) -> Response,
) -> Route {
    Route {
        method: method,
        pattern: pattern,
        reach: .Rest,
        serve: (q: Request<Unrouted>, t: Bytes) -> responded(read(t), q, serve),
    }
}

/// A route whose PATTERN declares its reach: a last hole marked `...`
/// takes the rest, any other reaches its own width. The door a route
/// written as a declaration (`get "/static/{path...}" { … }`) comes
/// through, where the pattern is the whole declaration.
export fn declared<R, T: Respond>(
    method: Method,
    pattern: string,
    read: fn(Bytes) -> R?,
    serve: fn(Request<R>) -> T,
) -> Route {
    Route {
        method: method,
        pattern: pattern,
        reach: if marks_rest(pattern) { .Rest } else { .Width },
        serve: (q: Request<Unrouted>, t: Bytes) -> responded(read(t), q, serve),
    }
}

// ── Routes as declarations ──────────────────────────────────────

// `get "/ideas/{id}" { req -> … }` — a route of one method at a
// pattern: the pattern mints its record, and the handler takes the
// request under it (`req.params.id`), or its fields by name
// (`{ params -> params.id }`).

export component get(path: Format) {
    body: Code
    fn expand() -> Code {
        quote { declared(Method.Get, ${self.path}.pattern(), (t: Bytes) -> ${self.path}.parse(t), ${self.body}) }
    }
}

export component post(path: Format) {
    body: Code
    fn expand() -> Code {
        quote { declared(Method.Post, ${self.path}.pattern(), (t: Bytes) -> ${self.path}.parse(t), ${self.body}) }
    }
}

export component put(path: Format) {
    body: Code
    fn expand() -> Code {
        quote { declared(Method.Put, ${self.path}.pattern(), (t: Bytes) -> ${self.path}.parse(t), ${self.body}) }
    }
}

export component patch(path: Format) {
    body: Code
    fn expand() -> Code {
        quote { declared(Method.Patch, ${self.path}.pattern(), (t: Bytes) -> ${self.path}.parse(t), ${self.body}) }
    }
}

/// `group "/api" { get "/ideas" { … } }` — routes under a prefix. Its
/// body lists routes, groups and routers in the order they answer.
export component group(prefix: Code) {
    body: Code
    fn expand() -> Code {
        quote { Mounted { found: prefixed(${self.prefix}, gathered(collect { ${self.body} })) } }
    }
}

/// `router tasks { get "/" { … } }` — a named group of routes with no
/// prefix, declared once and listed by its name wherever routes go: in a
/// server, a group or another router.
export component router {
    lines: List<dyn Routes> = []
}

impl Routes for router {
    fn routes() -> List<Route> { gathered(self.lines) }
}

/// What a body of routes may list: a route, a group, a router. The body
/// lists them in the order they answer.
export trait Routes {
    fn routes() -> List<Route>
}

impl Routes for Route {
    fn routes() -> List<Route> { [self] }
}

/// A group's routes, already under its prefix.
export type Mounted = { found: List<Route> }

impl Routes for Mounted {
    fn routes() -> List<Route> { self.found }
}

/// A list of routes as one item of a body of routes: `server app { mounted(routes) }`.
export fn mounted(routes: List<Route>) -> Mounted { Mounted { found: routes } }

/// The routes a body lists, in order, each item standing for its own.
export fn gathered(items: List<dyn Routes>) -> List<Route> {
    mut all: List<Route> = []
    for item in items { all = all.concat(item.routes()) }
    all
}

export component delete(path: Format) {
    body: Code
    fn expand() -> Code {
        quote { declared(Method.Delete, ${self.path}.pattern(), (t: Bytes) -> ${self.path}.parse(t), ${self.body}) }
    }
}

/// Routes under a prefix: each pattern begins with it, and each route
/// reads what follows it in the target.
export fn prefixed(prefix: string, routes: List<Route>) -> List<Route> { [placed(prefix, r) for r in routes] }

/// A route under a prefix. A route's own `/` is the prefix itself — a
/// collection lives at `/tasks`, and `/tasks/` is another target nothing answers.
fn placed(prefix: string, r: Route) -> Route {
    if r.pattern == "/" && !prefix.is_empty() {
        return r with { pattern: prefix, serve: exactly(prefix.bytes(), r.serve) }
    }
    r with { pattern: prefix + r.pattern, serve: beneath(prefix.bytes(), r.serve) }
}

/// `serve`, asked about the prefix alone as the root door it is.
fn exactly(prefix: Bytes, serve: fn(Request<Unrouted>, Bytes) -> Response?) -> fn(Request<Unrouted>, Bytes) -> Response? {
    (q: Request<Unrouted>, t: Bytes) -> if t == prefix { serve(q, "/".bytes()) } else { null }
}

/// A route's reader past a prefix — absent for a target that is not
/// beneath it, segment by segment.
fn beneath(prefix: Bytes, serve: fn(Request<Unrouted>, Bytes) -> Response?) -> fn(Request<Unrouted>, Bytes) -> Response? {
    (q: Request<Unrouted>, t: Bytes) -> if falls_under(t, prefix) { serve(q, t.slice(prefix.length, t.length)) } else { null }
}

/// The handler's answer as the reply it becomes, or absence when the
/// target is not this route's.
fn responded<R, T: Respond>(found: R?, q: Request<Unrouted>, serve: fn(Request<R>) -> T) -> Response? {
    let params? = found else { return null }
    serve(
        Request { raw: q.raw, head: q.head, body: q.body, peer: q.peer, params: params, set: q.set },
    )
        .response()
}

// ── Segments ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Where each segment of `b` ends — one entry per segment, always at
/// least one, the last past the final octet.
fn cuts(b: Bytes) -> List<int> { [i for i in 0..b.length + 1 if separates(b, i)] }

/// Where the segment ending at `cs[j]` begins.
fn opened(cs: List<int>, j: int) -> int { if j == 0 { 0 } else { cs[j - 1] + 1 } }

/// How many `/`-separated segments a target or a pattern has. The
/// table holds this law structurally — a route is only ever offered a
/// target of its own width — and this states it for a caller who
/// wants to ask.
export fn segments(b: Bytes) -> int { cuts(b).length }

/// A pattern's segments as text, cut where the table cuts them.
fn pieces(pattern: string) -> List<string> {
    let cs = cuts(pattern.bytes())
    [pattern.substring(opened(cs, j), c) for j, c in cs]
}

/// One octet folded into FNV-1a.
fn mixed(h: int, octet: int) -> int { (h ^ octet).wrapping_mul(1099511628211) }

fn seed() -> int { 0 - 3750763034362895579 }

/// The fold over one span of octets.
fn hashed(b: Bytes, from: int, to: int) -> int {
    mut h = seed()
    mut i = from
    // LICENSED loops.push_loop: a fold over octets, one runtime call per byte
    while i < to {
        h = mixed(h, b.at(i))
        i = i + 1
    }
    h
}

// ── The compiled table ──────────────────────────────────────────

/// One segment of a pattern: a LITERAL, keyed by its octets' hash, or
/// a HOLE. `key` is read only when `hole` is false, and `tail` only
/// ever marks the LAST segment of a route that takes the rest.
///
/// `rank` IS HOW GENERAL THE SEGMENT IS, and it is what the most
/// specific route answers by: 0 a literal, 1 a hole whose type is
/// NARROW — it refuses spans any text takes — 2 a hole any span
/// answers, 3 the rest. A hole's rank is its TYPE'S, so the ladder is
/// the type ladder: `7` is a literal, `{id: int}` a number, `{slug}`
/// any text, and `{path...}` a remainder.
type Seg = { hole: bool, key: int, tail: bool, rank: int }

/// Where a shape's tail begins, or -1 when it has none.
fn tail_at(shape: List<Seg>) -> int {
    if shape.last()!.tail { return shape.length - 1 }
    0 - 1
}

/// One trie node. `rows` are the routes that END here, MOST SPECIFIC
/// FIRST — which is what makes the table answer what a scan down the
/// same list would, and it does not depend on the order the routes were
/// written. `keys` is sorted ascending and `kids` is parallel to it, so
/// a step is a binary search over unboxed ints; `wild` is the step for
/// a segment no key names, and is -1 when no route has a hole here.
type Node = { rows: List<int>, keys: List<int>, kids: List<int>, wild: int }

/// A ROUTE TABLE COMPILED ONCE. Nothing here narrows wrongly: a route
/// is dropped only where a LITERAL segment differs from the target's,
/// which no reader could have matched. Everything that widens the
/// candidate set — a hole, two literals whose hashes collide — costs
/// a parse the reader then refuses, and never an answer.
///
/// A ROUTE THAT TAKES THE REST answers at every depth past its own,
/// so it is CARRIED DOWN the trie at build time and merged into every
/// deeper node's rows by specificity, as every node's rows are.
/// Nothing accumulates at dispatch, and where a target outruns the trie
/// entirely the walk lands on a node that answers the carried routes
/// and steps to itself.
export type Table = { routes: List<Route>, nodes: List<Node>, root: int }

/// Compile the table. Belongs in a `once fn` — the cost is paid at
/// the first request and never again.
///
/// THE NODES ARE BUILT BEFORE THE TABLE HOLDS THEM. `built` fills the
/// list through its seat, so a literal reading that list beside the
/// call would answer whatever the field order happened to be.
export fn compiled(routes: List<Route>) -> Table {
    mut nodes: List<Node> = []
    let root = built(nodes, [shaped(r) for r in routes], [i for i in 0..routes.length], 0, [])
    Table { routes: routes, nodes: nodes, root: root }
}

/// The node for `cands` at depth `d`, its children built first.
/// `carried` are the routes that take the rest and reached their tail
/// ABOVE this depth, so they answer here and at every depth below.
/// Every list is filtered from the candidates the caller ordered, so
/// every node's rows are ranked once, by `most_specific_first`.
///
/// A LITERAL KID CARRIES THE HOLES TOO: a route with a hole at `d`
/// matches that literal segment as well, so it rides both branches
/// and a step that finds a key needs no second look.
fn built(
    mut nodes: List<Node>,
    shapes: List<List<Seg>>,
    cands: List<int>,
    d: int,
    carried: List<int>,
) -> int {
    let down = sorted_distinct(carried.concat([r for r in cands if tail_at(shapes[r]) == d]))
    let live = [r for r in cands if shapes[r].length > d && tail_at(shapes[r]) != d]
    let segs = [shapes[r][d] for r in live]
    let keys = sorted_distinct([s.key for s in segs if !s.hole])
    let kids = [built(nodes, shapes, [live[j] for j, s in segs if s.hole || s.key == k], d + 1, down) for k in keys]
    let holes = [live[j] for j, s in segs if s.hole]
    nodes
        .push(
            Node {
                rows: most_specific_first(
                    shapes,
                    sorted_distinct(carried.concat([r for r in cands if shapes[r].length == d])),
                ),
                keys: keys,
                kids: kids,
                wild: wild_of(nodes, shapes, holes, d, down),
            },
        )
    nodes.length - 1
}

/// Routes ordered for answering: a route whose segments are more
/// specific answers first — at the first segment where two differ, a
/// literal before a hole before the rest — and declaration order breaks
/// only a true tie. So embedding order never changes which route answers.
fn most_specific_first(shapes: List<List<Seg>>, rows: List<int>) -> List<int> {
    mut out: List<int> = []
    // LICENSED loops.push_loop: an insertion sort folds its answer back in
    for r in rows {
        let at = [x for x in out if !tighter(shapes[r], shapes[x])].length
        out = out.slice(0, at).concat([r]).concat(out.slice(at, out.length))
    }
    out
}

/// Whether route `a` is strictly more specific than `b`, segment by
/// segment from the first.
fn tighter(a: List<Seg>, b: List<Seg>) -> bool {
    let shared = if a.length < b.length { a.length } else { b.length }
    let at = [i for i in 0..shared].find(a[it].rank != b[it].rank)
    if at == null { return false }
    a[at!].rank < b[at!].rank
}

/// The step for a segment no key names: the holes' subtree, else a
/// node answering the carried routes forever, else nothing.
fn wild_of(
    mut nodes: List<Node>,
    shapes: List<List<Seg>>,
    holes: List<int>,
    d: int,
    down: List<int>,
) -> int {
    if !holes.is_empty() { return built(nodes, shapes, holes, d + 1, down) }
    if down.is_empty() { return 0 - 1 }
    resting(nodes, down)
}

/// A node that answers `down` and steps to ITSELF — where a target
/// outruns every literal route and only the rest-takers remain.
fn resting(mut nodes: List<Node>, down: List<int>) -> int {
    nodes.push(Node { rows: down, keys: [], kids: [], wild: nodes.length })
    nodes.length - 1
}

/// The distinct values of `xs`, ascending — what makes a node's keys
/// answerable by binary search. Built once per node, at compile time.
fn sorted_distinct(xs: List<int>) -> List<int> {
    mut out: List<int> = []
    // LICENSED loops.push_loop: an insertion sort folds its answer back in
    for x in xs {
        if !out.contains(x) { out = inserted(out, x) }
    }
    out
}

fn inserted(xs: List<int>, x: int) -> List<int> {
    let at = [i for i in 0..xs.length].find(xs[it] > x) ?? xs.length
    xs.slice(0, at).concat([x]).concat(xs.slice(at, xs.length))
}

/// A route's segments. In a GRAMMAR pattern a segment holding a `{`
/// is a HOLE — the reader owns what is around it, and the table stops
/// keying on a segment it cannot know. In a STATIC path a brace is an
/// octet like any other and the segment keys exactly.
fn shaped(r: Route) -> List<Seg> {
    let b = r.pattern.bytes()
    let cs = cuts(b)
    let ps = pieces(r.pattern)
    [seg_of(b, ps[j], opened(cs, j), c, r.reach, j == cs.length - 1) for j, c in cs]
}

/// One segment, keyed by its octets and ranked by its type.
fn seg_of(b: Bytes, seg: string, from: int, to: int, reach: Reach, last: bool) -> Seg {
    let tail = last && takes_rest(reach)
    if holes_bind(reach) && holed(b, from, to) {
        let rank = when {
            tail -> 3
            narrow_span(seg) -> 1
            _ -> 2
        }
        return Seg { hole: true, key: 0, tail: tail, rank: rank }
    }
    Seg { hole: false, key: hashed(b, from, to), tail: tail, rank: if tail { 3 } else { 0 } }
}

/// Whether a segment's hole reads a NARROW span — its type refuses
/// what any text takes. A format's hole reads its span as `string` or
/// as `int`, so `int` is the whole narrow class: `{id: int}` outranks
/// `{slug}` and `{slug: string}`, which answer every span alike.
///
/// IT READS THE SEGMENT'S SPELLING, because a pattern's text is all a
/// route carries of its grammar — the same text the table keys its
/// literals by.
fn narrow_span(seg: string) -> bool {
    let open = seg.index_of("{")
    let shut = seg.index_of("}")
    if open < 0 || shut < open { return false }
    let body = seg.substring(open + 1, shut)
    let colon = body.index_of(":")
    if colon < 0 { return false }
    body.substring(colon + 1, body.length).trim() == "int"
}

fn holed(b: Bytes, from: int, to: int) -> bool {
    let brace = b.index_of(open_brace(), from)
    brace >= 0 && brace < to
}

once fn open_brace() -> Bytes { "{".bytes() }

// ── The dispatch ────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// THE DISPATCH: the first route the table still offers whose method
/// and target both answer.
///
/// The target is the FRAMED SPAN, never a string: the framer's
/// boundary holds to the door, and a grammar reads octets. A target
/// that is not UTF-8 matches no route and earns a 404 rather than a
/// 400 — the framer ADMITS `obs-text`, so such a target is well
/// formed and simply unrouted. A target nothing answers is a 404
/// either way, which is a VALUE: the router refuses nothing here.
///
/// A TARGET WITH NO PATH IS THE SAME 404, and the table is not walked
/// at all: `OPTIONS *` and CONNECT's authority name no resource, so
/// there is nothing to match them against.
///
/// A HEAD NO HEAD ROUTE ANSWERS IS ITS GET (RFC 9110 §9.3.2): the GET
/// route answers it, and the server writes that answer's head alone.
export fn dispatch(t: Table, q: Request<Unrouted>) -> Response {
    let target? = q.path() else { return status(404) }
    let node = landed(t, target)
    if node < 0 { return status(404) }
    served_as(t, node, q, target, q.head.method) ?? as_get(t, node, q, target) ?? status(404)
}

/// The first route at `node` declared for `m` that answers.
fn served_as(t: Table, node: int, q: Request<Unrouted>, target: Bytes, m: Method) -> Response? {
    for i in t.nodes[node].rows {
        let r = t.routes[i]
        if r.method == m {
            let answer: Response? = r.serve(q, target)
            if answer != null { return answer }
        }
    }
    null
}

/// A HEAD that no HEAD route answers is answered as its GET: every
/// general-purpose server supports both (RFC 9110 §9.1), and the loop
/// sends the length and none of the body.
fn as_get(t: Table, node: int, q: Request<Unrouted>, target: Bytes) -> Response? {
    if q.head.method != Method.Head { return null }
    served_as(t, node, q, target, Method.Get)
}

/// The node a target lands on, or -1 when the walk falls off the
/// table. ONE PASS over the octets, allocating nothing: a segment's
/// hash is folded as the walk crosses it and spent at its end.
fn landed(t: Table, b: Bytes) -> int {
    mut node = t.root
    mut h = seed()
    mut i = 0
    // LICENSED loops.push_loop: a descent folds over octets and stops early
    while i <= b.length {
        if separates(b, i) {
            node = step_to(t.nodes[node], h)
            if node < 0 { return 0 - 1 }
            h = seed()
        } else {
            h = mixed(h, b.at(i))
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    node
}

/// One step: the kid whose key is `key`, else the hole's kid, else -1.
fn step_to(n: Node, key: int) -> int {
    mut lo = 0
    mut hi = n.keys.length
    // LICENSED loops.push_loop: a binary search halves, it does not walk elements
    while lo < hi {
        let mid = (lo + hi) / 2
        if n.keys[mid] == key { return n.kids[mid] }
        if n.keys[mid] < key { lo = mid + 1 } else { hi = mid }
    }
    n.wild
}

// ── The law over a table ────────────────────────────────────────

/// WHAT IS WRONG WITH A TABLE: a mistake of the program's declarations,
/// decidable from the routes alone and quiet in a running server.
///
/// AN OVERLAP: two routes of ONE SPECIFICITY that may answer a common
/// target, so written order decides between them for the targets both
/// match. Where the earlier matches every target the later does, the
/// later never answers; where the two only meet on some, the later
/// answers the rest, and order still answers for the meeting. A more
/// specific route is no overlap — it answers first wherever it stands.
///
/// A POSITION'S RANK IS ITS TYPE'S, so two holes of one rank are only
/// ever told apart by order: `{id: int}` against `{num: int}` is not
/// two routes, and `v{n: int}` against `{n: int}` is one route that
/// is partly unreachable. Only LITERALS can be told disjoint — a
/// hole's span is not a set a declaration can enumerate — so this law
/// refuses what it cannot prove apart rather than inventing a
/// disjointness no reader could check.
///
/// A REST DISAGREEMENT: `tailed` promises the remaining segments to a
/// hole and the pattern must say so with `...`; `routed` promises one
/// width and its pattern must not.
export enum RouteFault {
    Overlap(first: Route, second: Route)
    Unmarked(route: Route)
    Unclaimed(route: Route)
}

/// Every fault of a table, in declaration order.
export fn route_faults(routes: List<Route>) -> List<RouteFault> {
    [f for j in 0..routes.length for f in faults_of(routes, j)]
}

/// The faults route `j` brings to the table.
fn faults_of(routes: List<Route>, j: int) -> List<RouteFault> {
    let r = routes[j]
    let above = overshadowed_by(routes, j)
    let unmarked: RouteFault? = if takes_rest(r.reach) && !marks_rest(r.pattern) { .Unmarked(r) } else { null }
    let unclaimed: RouteFault? = if r.reach == Reach.Width && marks_rest(r.pattern) { .Unclaimed(r) } else { null }
    let overlap: RouteFault? = if above != null { .Overlap(routes[above!], r) } else { null }
    [f! for f in [unmarked, unclaimed, overlap] if f != null]
}

/// A table's faults in words, one per fault.
export fn faults(routes: List<Route>) -> List<string> {
    [told(f) for f in route_faults(routes)]
}

fn overshadowed_by(routes: List<Route>, j: int) -> int? {
    [k for k in 0..j].find(shadows(routes[it], routes[j]))
}

/// Whether a pattern's LAST segment carries a greedy hole. Anywhere
/// in that segment counts — `{path...}` and `{path...}.png` both take
/// the rest, the second anchoring its own end.
fn marks_rest(pattern: string) -> bool { pieces(pattern).last()!.contains(rest_mark()) }

/// The format's greedy mark, closing its hole.
fn rest_mark() -> string { "...}" }

fn shadows(a: Route, b: Route) -> bool {
    if a.method != b.method { return false }
    let x = shaped(a)
    let y = shaped(b)
    alike(x, y) && coincide(x, y)
}

/// Whether two routes are equally specific, segment for segment: only then
/// does order decide between them.
fn alike(a: List<Seg>, b: List<Seg>) -> bool {
    a.length == b.length && [i for i in 0..a.length].all(a[it].rank == b[it].rank)
}

/// Whether two equally specific routes may answer one target. Only two
/// LITERALS can be disjoint, and only by differing; a hole of either
/// rank is taken to meet every hole of its own rank, since nothing a
/// declaration says proves what spans a type refuses.
fn coincide(a: List<Seg>, b: List<Seg>) -> bool {
    [i for i in 0..a.length].all(a[it].hole || b[it].hole || a[it].key == b[it].key)
}

/// Whether every target `b` matches, `a` matches too — asked segment
/// by segment, over the same segments the table descends.
///
/// A STATIC path covers one target and nothing else, braces included,
/// so it covers another route only by being the same path. A
/// REST-TAKING route covers every width past its own, so it needs
/// only its prefix to cover and a last segment that answers anything;
/// and no route of fixed width can cover one that takes the rest,
/// because there is always a deeper target it does not reach.
fn covers(a: Route, b: Route) -> bool {
    if a.reach == Reach.Literal { return b.reach == Reach.Literal && a.pattern == b.pattern }
    if takes_rest(b.reach) && !takes_rest(a.reach) { return false }
    let x = pieces(a.pattern)
    let y = pieces(b.pattern)
    if takes_rest(a.reach) { return y.length >= x.length && open_hole(x.last()!) && covers_upto(x, y, x.length - 1) }
    x.length == y.length && covers_upto(x, y, x.length)
}

/// Whether `a`'s first `n` segments each cover `b`'s.
fn covers_upto(x: List<string>, y: List<string>, n: int) -> bool {
    [covers_seg(x[j], y[j]) for j in 0..n].all(it)
}

fn covers_seg(a: string, b: string) -> bool { open_hole(a) || a == b }

/// A segment that is exactly one hole binding a name and NO TYPE —
/// `{id}` — which every segment answers.
///
/// IT ASKS ABOUT THE TYPE RATHER THAN ASSUMING THERE IS NONE. A hole
/// that reads its span (`{id: int}`) can REFUSE one, so it covers no
/// segment but its own spelling; a hole beside a literal (`v{major}`)
/// covers only what that literal admits. Both are excluded by the
/// rule itself, which is what keeps this the COVERAGE question —
/// whether the later route is answered by nothing at all.
fn open_hole(seg: string) -> bool {
    seg.starts_with("{") &&
        seg.index_of("}") == seg.length - 1 &&
        !seg.contains(":") &&
        !seg.substring(1, seg.length).contains("{")
}

// ── Voices ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// A fault's voice.
export fn told(f: RouteFault) -> string {
    match f {
        .Overlap(first, second) -> clash(first, second),
        .Unmarked(r) -> unmarked(r),
        .Unclaimed(r) -> unclaimed(r),
    }
}

/// An overlap's voice, by whether the earlier route answers EVERY
/// target the later does: then the later is answered by nothing, and
/// otherwise the two meet on some.
fn clash(earlier: Route, later: Route) -> string {
    if covers(earlier, later) { unreachable(later, earlier) } else { shared(earlier, later) }
}

/// A route by method and pattern.
fn named_route(r: Route) -> string { "${method_word(r.method) ?? "?"} ${r.pattern}" }

fn unreachable(later: Route, earlier: Route) -> string {
    "`${named_route(later)}` is unreachable — `${named_route(earlier)}` is declared above it and matches every target it does"
}

fn shared(earlier: Route, later: Route) -> string {
    "`${named_route(later)}` and `${named_route(earlier)}` are equally specific and match some of the same targets — nothing but written order would separate them; narrow one, or drop one"
}

fn unmarked(r: Route) -> string {
    "`${named_route(r)}` is declared to take the rest and its last hole does not say so — write `{name...}`, which is what its grammar spells too"
}

fn unclaimed(r: Route) -> string {
    "`${named_route(r)}` spells a hole that takes the rest and the route was declared for one width — declare it with `tailed`, or drop the `...`"
}
